{
  "rules": [
    "Downtrend bias: in falling regimes the model is consistently too high by ~0.2–3 EUR, with the largest positive errors around the local trough (days ~8–15). Apply a negative level shift when recent 10–15d momentum is negative.",
    "Amplitude/overshoot error: the model overestimates rebounds after sell-offs (predicts stronger bounce than realized). Damp positive day-to-day changes following a multi-day decline (e.g., scale rebound magnitude down).",
    "Horizon shape mismatch: short-horizon (days 1–5) is relatively close, but mid-horizon (days ~6–20) drifts too high versus realized path. Increase mean-reversion strength toward the recent realized trend for days 6–20.",
    "Regime-change lag: when the market turns sharply (e.g., late Example 3 where price rallies), the model remains anchored to the prior bearish regime and underpredicts the late-horizon move. Add a trend-flip detector (e.g., 3–5d breakout above recent range) that lifts days 20–30 forecasts.",
    "Volatility underreaction at extremes: realized moves at turning points can be larger than forecast (late rally), while forecasted swings during declines are too smooth/optimistic. Use asymmetric adjustments: stronger downward correction in downtrends, but allow higher upside tail when a breakout signal triggers."
  ]
}
